  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1241 PM CDT THU AUG 27 2026\n\nRidging across the southwest remains a big driver in the forecast\nover here in Alabama, with a ~596 dm ridge centered over Arizona\nand New Mexico. A weak front is currently draped across Central\nAlabama, with shower activity ongoing across southern parts of\nthe state. Another front is approaching from the northwest,\ncurrently over Arkansas and Kentucky. This will slide in to\nreplace as the current boundary dissipates. This afternoon, we\nexpect more in the way of showers and thunderstorms to develop\nacross mainly southern and southeastern portions of the forecast\narea, to the south of the current boundary. Thicker cloud cover\nin spots today may keep highs in the mid 80s. A similar situation\nwill develop on Friday, as storms develop mainly along a line\nfrom Selma to Anniston to NE Georgia. A level 1 Marginal risk for\nsevere thunderstorms is currently in effect for parts of Georgia,\nbut does not extend into Alabama with this forecast update. Any\nstorms that develop will likely be of the routine summer variety,\nand could cause instances of minor wind damage.\n\nAs the ridge slides eastward over the next few days, we will see\ntemperatures heat back up from the upper 80s/low 90s to the mid\n90s by the middle of next week. Rain chances from Saturday onward\nare lower than the next couple days at this point, and those\nchances will remain concentrated mainly across southern and\nsoutheastern areas for the next few days.\n\n12",
